#. type: TH
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "MPSTAT"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "JULY 2025"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:5
msgid "mpstat - Report processors related statistics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:10
msgid ""
"B<mpstat [ -A ] [ --dec={ 0 | 1 | 2 } ] [ -H ] [ -n ] [ -U ] [ -u ] [ -T ] [ "
"-V ] [ -I {> I<keyword>B<[,...] | ALL } ] [ -N { >I<node_list >B<| ALL } ] [ "
"-o JSON ] [ -P {> I<cpu_list >B<| ALL } ] [ >I<interval >B<[ >I<count >B<] "
"]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:20
msgid ""
"The B<mpstat> command writes to standard output activities for each "
"available processor, processor 0 being the first one.  Global average "
"activities among all processors are also reported.  The B<mpstat> command "
"can be used on both SMP and UP machines, but in the latter, only global "
"average activities will be printed. If no activity has been selected, then "
"the default report is the CPU utilization report."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:34
msgid ""
"The I<interval> parameter specifies the amount of time in seconds between "
"each report.  A value of 0 (or no parameters at all) indicates that "
"processors statistics are to be reported for the time since system startup "
"(boot). The I<count >parameter can be specified in conjunction with the "
"I<interval> parameter if this one is not set to zero. The value of I<count> "
"determines the number of reports generated at I<interval >seconds apart. If "
"the I<interval> parameter is specified without the I<count >parameter, the "
"B<mpstat> command generates reports continuously."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:36 original/man1/sar.1:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-A>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:43
msgid ""
"This option is equivalent to specifying B<-n -u -I ALL>.  This option also "
"implies specifying B<-N ALL -P ALL> unless these options are explicitly set "
"on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:49
msgid "Also detect and display statistics for physically hotplugged vCPUs."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-I { >I<keyword>B<[,...] | ALL }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:54
msgid ""
"Report interrupts statistics.  Possible I<keywords >are B<CPU>, B<SCPU>, and "
"B<SUM>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:60
msgid ""
"With the B<CPU> keyword, the number of each individual interrupt received "
"per second by the CPU or CPUs is displayed. Interrupts are those listed in "
"I</proc/interrupts >file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:66
msgid ""
"With the B<SCPU> keyword, the number of each individual software interrupt "
"received per second by the CPU or CPUs is displayed. This option works only "
"with kernels 2.6.31 and later. Software interrupts are those listed in "
"I</proc/softirqs >file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:70
msgid ""
"With the B<SUM >keyword, the B<mpstat> command reports the total number of "
"interrupts per processor.  The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:71 original/man1/mpstat.1:143
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:294
#, no-wrap
msgid "CPU"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:75
msgid ""
"Processor number. The keyword B<all> indicates that statistics are "
"calculated as averages among all processors."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "intr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:79
msgid "Show the total number of interrupts received per second by the CPU or CPUs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:84
msgid ""
"The B<ALL> keyword is equivalent to specifying all the keywords above and "
"therefore all the interrupts statistics are displayed."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:84
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-N { >I<node_list >B<| ALL }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:93
msgid ""
"Indicate the NUMA nodes for which statistics are to be reported.  "
"I<node_list> is a list of comma-separated values or range of values (e.g., "
"B<0,2,4-7,12->). Note that node B<all> is the global average among all "
"nodes. The B<ALL> keyword indicates that statistics are to be reported for "
"all nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:93
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-n>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:97
msgid ""
"Report summary CPU statistics based on NUMA node placement. The following "
"values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:98 original/man1/mpstat.1:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "NODE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:102
msgid ""
"Logical NUMA node number. The keyword B<all> indicates that statistics are "
"calculated as averages among all nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:106
msgid ""
"All the other fields are the same as those displayed with option B<-u >(see "
"below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:111 original/man1/sadf.1:227 original/man1/sar.1:1268
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-P { >I<cpu_list >B<| ALL }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:123
msgid ""
"Indicate the processors for which statistics are to be reported.  "
"I<cpu_list> is a list of comma-separated values or range of values (e.g., "
"B<0,2,4-7,12->).  Note that processor 0 is the first processor, and "
"processor B<all> is the global average among all processors.  The B<ALL> "
"keyword indicates that statistics are to be reported for all processors.  "
"Offline processors are not displayed."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:123 original/man1/sadf.1:266
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-T>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:128
msgid ""
"Display topology elements in the CPU report (see option B<-u> below). The "
"following elements are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:129
#, no-wrap
msgid "CORE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:131
msgid "Logical core number."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "SOCK"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:133
msgid "Logical socket number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:135
msgid "Logical NUMA node number."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:139 original/man1/pidstat.1:266
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-u>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:142
msgid "Report CPU utilization. The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:147
msgid ""
"Processor number. The keyword I<all> indicates that statistics are "
"calculated as averages among all processors."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:147 original/man1/pidstat.1:279
#: original/man1/sar.1:1519
#, no-wrap
msgid "%usr"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:153 original/man1/sar.1:1530
#, no-wrap
msgid "%sys"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:157
msgid ""
"Show the percentage of CPU utilization that occurred while executing at the "
"system level (kernel). Note that this does not include time spent servicing "
"hardware and software interrupts."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:160 original/man1/sar.1:1540
#, no-wrap
msgid "%irq"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:162
msgid ""
"Show the percentage of time spent by the CPU or CPUs to service hardware "
"interrupts."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:162 original/man1/sar.1:1542
#, no-wrap
msgid "%soft"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:164
msgid ""
"Show the percentage of time spent by the CPU or CPUs to service software "
"interrupts."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:167 original/man1/pidstat.1:285
#: original/man1/sar.1:1544
#, no-wrap
msgid "%guest"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:169
msgid ""
"Show the percentage of time spent by the CPU or CPUs to run a virtual "
"processor."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:169 original/man1/sar.1:1546
#, no-wrap
msgid "%gnice"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:171
msgid "Show the percentage of time spent by the CPU or CPUs to run a niced guest."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:182
msgid "The B<mpstat> command takes into account the following environment variable:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:202
msgid "SGR (Select Graphic Rendition) substring for CPU number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:225
msgid ""
"If this variable exists and its value is B<ISO> then the current locale will "
"be ignored when printing the date in the report header.  The B<mpstat> "
"command will use the ISO 8601 format (YYYY-MM-DD) instead.  The timestamp "
"will also be compliant with ISO 8601 format."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:227
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<mpstat 2 5>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:230
msgid ""
"Display five reports of global statistics among all processors at two second "
"intervals."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:230
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<mpstat -P ALL 2 5>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:233
msgid ""
"Display five reports of statistics for all processors at two second "
"intervals."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:237
msgid "I</proc >filesystem must be mounted for the B<mpstat >command to work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:240 original/man1/pidstat.1:450
msgid "I</proc >contains various files with system statistics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/mpstat.1:246
msgid "B<sar>(1), B<pidstat>(1), B<iostat>(1), B<vmstat>(8)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "PIDSTAT"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "JANUARY 2025"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:5
msgid "pidstat - Report statistics for Linux tasks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:13
msgid ""
"B<pidstat [ -d ] [ -H ] [ -h ] [ -I ] [ -l ] [ -R ] [ -r ] [ -s ] [ -t ] [ "
"-U [> I<username >B<] ] [ -u ] [ -V ] [ -v ] [ -w ] [ -C >I<comm >B<] [ -G "
">I<process_name> B<] [ --dec={ 0 | 1 | 2 } ] [ --human ] [ -o JSON ] [ -p { "
">I<pid>B<[,...]> B<| SELF | ALL } ] [ -T { TASK | CHILD | ALL } ] [> "
"I<interval >B<[ >I<count >B<] ] [ -e >I<program> I<args >B<]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:26
msgid ""
"The B<pidstat> command is used for monitoring individual tasks currently "
"being managed by the Linux kernel.  It writes to standard output activities "
"for every task selected with option B<-p> or for every task managed by the "
"Linux kernel if option B<-p ALL> has been used. Not selecting any tasks is "
"equivalent to specifying B<-p ALL> but only active tasks (tasks with "
"non-zero statistics values)  will appear in the report."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:31
msgid ""
"The B<pidstat> command can also be used for monitoring the child processes "
"of selected tasks.  Read about option B<-T >below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:43
msgid ""
"The I<interval> parameter specifies the amount of time in seconds between "
"each report.  A value of 0 (or no parameters at all) indicates that tasks "
"statistics are to be reported for the time since system startup (boot). The "
"I<count >parameter can be specified in conjunction with the I<interval "
">parameter if this one is not set to zero. The value of I<count >determines "
"the number of reports generated at I<interval >seconds apart. If the "
"I<interval >parameter is specified without the I<count> parameter, the "
"B<pidstat> command generates reports continuously."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:46
msgid ""
"You can select information about specific task activities using flags.  Not "
"specifying any flags selects only CPU activity."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:48
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-C >I<comm>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:52
msgid ""
"Display only tasks whose command name includes the string I<comm>. This "
"string can be a regular expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:56
msgid ""
"Report I/O statistics (kernels 2.6.20 and later only).  The following values "
"may be displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:57 original/man1/pidstat.1:146
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:166 original/man1/pidstat.1:192
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:216 original/man1/pidstat.1:273
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:303 original/man1/pidstat.1:336
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:354
#, no-wrap
msgid "UID"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:59 original/man1/pidstat.1:148
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:168 original/man1/pidstat.1:218
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:275 original/man1/pidstat.1:338
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:356
msgid "The real user identification number of the task being monitored."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:59 original/man1/pidstat.1:148
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:168 original/man1/pidstat.1:195
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:218 original/man1/pidstat.1:275
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:306 original/man1/pidstat.1:338
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:356
#, no-wrap
msgid "USER"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:61 original/man1/pidstat.1:150
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:170 original/man1/pidstat.1:220
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:277 original/man1/pidstat.1:340
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:358
msgid "The name of the real user owning the task being monitored."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:61 original/man1/pidstat.1:150
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:170 original/man1/pidstat.1:198
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:220 original/man1/pidstat.1:277
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:309 original/man1/pidstat.1:340
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:358
#, no-wrap
msgid "PID"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:63 original/man1/pidstat.1:152
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:172 original/man1/pidstat.1:222
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:279 original/man1/pidstat.1:342
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:360
msgid "The identification number of the task being monitored."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:63
#, no-wrap
msgid "kB_rd/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:65
msgid "Number of kibibytes the task has caused to be read from disk per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "kB_wr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:68
msgid ""
"Number of kibibytes the task has caused, or shall cause to be written to "
"disk per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "kB_ccwr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:73
msgid ""
"Number of kibibytes whose writing to disk has been cancelled by the "
"task. This may occur when the task truncates some dirty pagecache. In this "
"case, some IO which another task has been accounted for will not be "
"happening."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "iodelay"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:78
msgid ""
"Block I/O delay of the task being monitored, measured in clock ticks. This "
"metric includes the delays spent waiting for sync block I/O completion and "
"for swapin block I/O completion."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:78 original/man1/pidstat.1:156
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:185 original/man1/pidstat.1:207
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:227 original/man1/pidstat.1:296
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:325 original/man1/pidstat.1:346
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:369
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:80 original/man1/pidstat.1:158
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:187 original/man1/pidstat.1:229
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:298 original/man1/pidstat.1:348
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:371
msgid "The command name of the task."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:84
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-e >I<program args>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:96
msgid ""
"Execute I<program> with given arguments I<args> and monitor it with "
"B<pidstat>.  B<pidstat >stops when I<program >terminates.  A non-zero value "
"must be specified for the I<interval >parameter."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-G >I<process_name>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:105
msgid ""
"Display only processes whose command name includes the string "
"I<process_name>.  This string can be a regular expression. If option B<-t "
">is used together with option B<-G> then the threads belonging to that "
"process are also displayed (even if their command name doesn't include the "
"string I<process_name>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:108
msgid "Display timestamp in seconds since the epoch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:113
msgid ""
"Display all activities horizontally on a single line, with no average "
"statistics at the end of the report. This is intended to make it easier to "
"be parsed by other programs."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-I>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:123
msgid ""
"In an SMP environment, indicate that tasks CPU usage (as displayed by option "
"B<-u>)  should be divided by the total number of processors."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:123 original/man1/sadf.1:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-l>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:126
msgid "Display the process command name and all its arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-p { >I<pid>B<[,...] | SELF | ALL }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:141
msgid ""
"Select tasks (processes) for which statistics are to be reported.  I<pid> is "
"the process identification number. The B<SELF> keyword indicates that "
"statistics are to be reported for the B<pidstat >process itself, whereas the "
"B<ALL> keyword indicates that statistics are to be reported for all the "
"tasks managed by the system."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:141
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-R>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:145
msgid ""
"Report realtime priority and scheduling policy information.  The following "
"values may be displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:152
#, no-wrap
msgid "prio"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:154
msgid "The realtime priority of the task being monitored."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:154
#, no-wrap
msgid "policy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:156
msgid "The scheduling policy of the task being monitored."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:159 original/man1/sadf.1:250
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-r>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:162
msgid "Report page faults and memory utilization."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:165 original/man1/pidstat.1:272
msgid ""
"When reporting statistics for individual tasks, the following values may be "
"displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:172
#, no-wrap
msgid "minflt/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:175
msgid ""
"Total number of minor faults the task has made per second, those which have "
"not required loading a memory page from disk."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:175 original/man1/sar.1:145
#, no-wrap
msgid "majflt/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:178
msgid ""
"Total number of major faults the task has made per second, those which have "
"required loading a memory page from disk."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:178
#, no-wrap
msgid "VSZ"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:180
msgid "Virtual Size: The virtual memory usage of entire task in kibibytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:180
#, no-wrap
msgid "RSS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:183
msgid ""
"Resident Set Size: The non-swapped physical memory used by the task in "
"kibibytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:183
#, no-wrap
msgid "%MEM"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:185
msgid "The tasks's currently used share of available physical memory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:191 original/man1/pidstat.1:302
msgid ""
"When reporting global statistics for tasks and all their children, the "
"following values may be displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:195 original/man1/pidstat.1:306
msgid ""
"The real user identification number of the task which is being monitored "
"together with its children."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:198 original/man1/pidstat.1:309
msgid ""
"The name of the real user owning the task which is being monitored together "
"with its children."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:201 original/man1/pidstat.1:312
msgid ""
"The identification number of the task which is being monitored together with "
"its children."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:201
#, no-wrap
msgid "minflt-nr"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:204
msgid ""
"Total number of minor faults made by the task and all its children, and "
"collected during the interval of time."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:204
#, no-wrap
msgid "majflt-nr"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:207
msgid ""
"Total number of major faults made by the task and all its children, and "
"collected during the interval of time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:210 original/man1/pidstat.1:328
msgid ""
"The command name of the task which is being monitored together with its "
"children."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:215
msgid "Report stack utilization.  The following values may be displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:222
#, no-wrap
msgid "StkSize"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:225
msgid ""
"The amount of memory in kibibytes reserved for the task as stack, but not "
"necessarily used."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:225
#, no-wrap
msgid "StkRef"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:227
msgid "The amount of memory in kibibytes used as stack, referenced by the task."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:230
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-T { TASK | CHILD | ALL }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:243
msgid ""
"This option specifies what has to be monitored by the B<pidstat "
">command. The B<TASK> keyword indicates that statistics are to be reported "
"for individual tasks (this is the default option) whereas the B<CHILD> "
"keyword indicates that statistics are to be globally reported for the "
"selected tasks and all their children. The B<ALL> keyword indicates that "
"statistics are to be reported for individual tasks and globally for the "
"selected tasks and their children."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:250
msgid ""
"Note: Global statistics for tasks and all their children are not available "
"for all options of B<pidstat.> Also these statistics are not necessarily "
"relevant to current time interval: The statistics of a child process are "
"collected only when it finishes or it is killed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:253
msgid "Also display statistics for threads associated with selected tasks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:255
msgid "This option adds the following values to the reports:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:256
#, no-wrap
msgid "TGID"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:258
msgid "The identification number of the thread group leader."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:258
#, no-wrap
msgid "TID"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:260
msgid "The identification number of the thread being monitored."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:261
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-U [ >I<username >B<]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:266
msgid ""
"Display the real user name of the tasks being monitored instead of the UID.  "
"If I<username> is specified, then only tasks belonging to the specified user "
"are displayed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:269
msgid "Report CPU utilization."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:283
msgid ""
"Percentage of CPU used by the task while executing at the user level "
"(application), with or without nice priority. Note that this field does NOT "
"include time spent running a virtual processor."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:285
msgid ""
"Percentage of CPU used by the task while executing at the system level "
"(kernel)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:287
msgid ""
"Percentage of CPU spent by the task in virtual machine (running a virtual "
"processor)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:287
#, no-wrap
msgid "%wait"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:289
msgid "Percentage of CPU spent by the task while waiting to run."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:289
#, no-wrap
msgid "%CPU"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:294
msgid ""
"Total percentage of CPU time used by the task. In an SMP environment, the "
"task's CPU usage will be divided by the total number of CPU's if option "
"B<-I> has been entered on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:296
msgid "Processor number to which the task is attached."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:312
#, no-wrap
msgid "usr-ms"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:318
msgid ""
"Total number of milliseconds spent by the task and all its children while "
"executing at the user level (application), with or without nice priority, "
"and collected during the interval of time. Note that this field does NOT "
"include time spent running a virtual processor."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:318
#, no-wrap
msgid "system-ms"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:322
msgid ""
"Total number of milliseconds spent by the task and all its children while "
"executing at the system level (kernel), and collected during the interval of "
"time."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:322
#, no-wrap
msgid "guest-ms"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:325
msgid ""
"Total number of milliseconds spent by the task and all its children in "
"virtual machine (running a virtual processor)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:332 original/man1/sar.1:1555
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-v>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:335
msgid "Report values of some kernel tables. The following values may be displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:342
#, no-wrap
msgid "threads"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:344
msgid "Number of threads associated with current task."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:344
#, no-wrap
msgid "fd-nr"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:346
msgid "Number of file descriptors associated with current task."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:349 original/man1/sar.1:1578
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-w>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:353
msgid ""
"Report task switching activity (kernels 2.6.23 and later only).  The "
"following values may be displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:360 original/man1/sar.1:1585
#, no-wrap
msgid "cswch/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:364
msgid ""
"Total number of voluntary context switches the task made per second.  A "
"voluntary context switch occurs when a task blocks because it requires a "
"resource that is unavailable."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:364
#, no-wrap
msgid "nvcswch/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:369
msgid ""
"Total number of non voluntary context switches the task made per second.  An "
"involuntary context switch takes place when a task executes for the duration "
"of its time slice and then is forced to relinquish the processor."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:377
msgid ""
"The B<pidstat> command takes into account the following environment "
"variables:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:397
msgid ""
"SGR (Select Graphic Rendition) substring for item values like PID, UID or "
"CPU number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:400
msgid "SGR substring for non-zero statistics values and for tasks names."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:411
msgid "SGR substring for zero values and for threads names."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:420
msgid ""
"If this variable exists and its value is B<ISO> then the current locale will "
"be ignored when printing the date in the report header. The B<pidstat> "
"command will use the ISO 8601 format (YYYY-MM-DD) instead.  The timestamp "
"will also be compliant with ISO 8601 format."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:422
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<pidstat 2 5>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:426
msgid ""
"Display five reports of CPU statistics for every active task in the system "
"at two second intervals."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:426
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<pidstat -r -p 1643 2 5>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:430
msgid ""
"Display five reports of page faults and memory statistics for PID 1643 at "
"two second intervals."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:430
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<pidstat -C \"fox|bird\" -r -p ALL>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:434
msgid ""
"Display global page faults and memory statistics for all the processes whose "
"command name includes the string \"fox\" or \"bird\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:434
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<pidstat -T CHILD -r 2 5>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:439
msgid ""
"Display five reports of page faults statistics at two second intervals for "
"the child processes of all tasks in the system. Only child processes with "
"non-zero statistics values are displayed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:443
msgid "I</proc >filesystem must be mounted for the B<pidstat >command to work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:447
msgid ""
"Although B<pidstat> displays units corresponding to kilobytes (kB), "
"megabytes (MB)..., it actually uses kibibytes (kiB), mebibytes (MiB)...  A "
"kibibyte is equal to 1024 bytes, and a mebibyte is equal to 1024 kibibytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/pidstat.1:456
msgid "B<sar>(1), B<top>(1), B<ps>(1), B<mpstat>(1), B<iostat>(1), B<vmstat>(8)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: original/man1/sadf.1:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "SADF"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:5
msgid "sadf - Display data collected by sar in multiple formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:12
msgid ""
"B<sadf [ -C ] [ -c | -d | -g | -j | -l | -p | -r | -x ] [ -H ] [ -h ] [ -T | "
"-t | -U ] [ -V ] [ -O> I<opts >B<[,...] ] [ -P { >I<cpu_list >B<| ALL } ] [ "
"-s [ > I<start_time >B<] ] ] [ -e [ >I<end_time >B<] ] ]> B<[ "
"--dev=>I<dev_list >B<] [ --fs=>I<fs_list >B<] [ --iface=>I<iface_list>B<] [ "
"--int=>I<int_list >B<] [ --> I<sar_options >B<] [ >I<interval >B<[ >I<count "
">B<] ] [ >I<datafile >B<| >I<-[0-9]+ >B<]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:26
msgid ""
"The B<sadf> command is used for displaying the contents of data files "
"created by the B<sar>(1) command. But unlike B<sar>, B<sadf> can write its "
"data in many different formats (CSV, XML, etc.)  The default format is one "
"that can easily be handled by pattern processing commands like B<awk >(see "
"option B<-p>). The B<sadf> command can also be used to draw graphs for the "
"various activities collected by B<sar> and display them as SVG (Scalable "
"Vector Graphics) graphics in your web browser (see option B<-g>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:40
msgid ""
"The B<sadf> command extracts and writes to standard output records saved in "
"the I<datafile> file. This file must have been created by a version of B<sar "
">which is compatible with that of B<sadf>. If I<datafile> is omitted, "
"B<sadf> uses the standard system activity daily data file.  It is also "
"possible to enter B<-1>, B<-2 >etc. as an argument to B<sadf> to display "
"data of that days ago. For example, B<-1> will point at the standard system "
"activity file of yesterday."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:54
msgid ""
"The standard system activity daily data file is named I<saDD >or "
"I<saYYYYMMDD>, where I<YYYY >stands for the current year, I<MM >for the "
"current month and I<DD> for the current day.  B<sadf> will look for the most "
"recent of I<saDD >and I<saYYYYMMDD>, and use it. By default it is located in "
"the I</var/log/sa> directory. Yet it is possible to specify an alternate "
"location for it: If I<datafile> is a directory (instead of a plain file) "
"then it will be considered as the directory where the standard system "
"activity daily data file is located."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:59
msgid ""
"The I<interval >and I<count >parameters are used to tell B<sadf >to select "
"I<count >records at I<interval >seconds apart. If the I<count> parameter is "
"not set, then all the records saved in the data file will be displayed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:68
msgid ""
"All the activity flags of B<sar> may be entered on the command line to "
"indicate which activities are to be reported. Before specifying them, put a "
"pair of dashes (B<-->)  on the command line in order not to confuse the "
"flags with those of B<sadf.> Not specifying any flags selects only CPU "
"activity."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:70 original/man1/sar.1:188
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-C>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:73
msgid "Tell B<sadf >to display comments present in file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:77
msgid ""
"Convert an old system activity binary datafile (version 9.1.6 and later)  to "
"current up-to-date format. Use the following syntax:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:79
msgid "B<sadf -c >I<old_datafile >B<E<gt> >I<new_datafile>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:82
msgid "Conversion can be controlled using option B<-O >(see below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:93
msgid ""
"Print the contents of the data file in a format that can easily be ingested "
"by a relational database system. The output consists of fields separated by "
"a semicolon. Each record contains the hostname of the host where the file "
"was created, the interval value (or -1 if not applicable), the timestamp in "
"a form easily acceptable by most databases, and additional semicolon "
"separated data fields as specified by I<sar_options >command line options.  "
"Note that timestamp output can be controlled by options B<-T>, B<-t >and "
"B<-U>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:93 original/man1/sar.1:251
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--dev=>I<dev_list>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:100
msgid ""
"Specify the block devices for which statistics are to be displayed by "
"B<sadf>.  I<dev_list> is a list of comma-separated device names. Useful with "
"option B<-d >from B<sar>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:103 original/man1/sar.1:259
msgid "B<-e [ >I<hh>B<:>I<mm>B<[:>I<ss>B<] ]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:105 original/man1/sar.1:261
msgid "B<-e [ >I<seconds_since_the_epoch >B<]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:109
msgid ""
"Set the ending time of the report. The default ending time is "
"18:00:00. Hours must be given in 24-hour format, or as the number of seconds "
"since the epoch (given as a 10 digit number)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:110 original/man1/sar.1:314
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--fs=>I<fs_list>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:118
msgid ""
"Specify the filesystems for which statistics are to be displayed by "
"B<sadf>.  I<fs_list> is a list of comma-separated filesystem device names "
"and/or mountpoints.  Either form is accepted for filtering. Useful with "
"option B<-F >from B<sar>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-g>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:123
msgid ""
"Print the contents of the data file in SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) "
"format.  This option enables you to display some fancy graphs in your web "
"browser.  Use the following syntax:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:125
msgid "B<sadf -g >I<your_datafile >B<[ -- >I<sar_options >B<] E<gt> >I<output.svg>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:129
msgid ""
"and open the resulting SVG file in your favorite web browser.  Output can be "
"controlled using option B<-O >(see below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:133
msgid ""
"Display only the header of the report (when applicable). If no format has "
"been specified, then the header data (metadata) of the data file are "
"displayed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:137
msgid ""
"When used in conjunction with option B<-d>, all activities will be displayed "
"horizontally on a single line."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:137 original/man1/sar.1:374
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--iface=>I<iface_list>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:144
msgid ""
"Specify the network interfaces for which statistics are to be displayed by "
"B<sadf>.  I<iface_list> is a list of comma-separated interface names. Useful "
"with options B<-n DEV >and B<-n EDEV >from B<sar>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:144 original/man1/sar.1:380
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--int=>I<int_list>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:151
msgid ""
"Specify the interrupts names for which statistics are to be displayed by "
"B<sadf>.  I<int_list> is a list of comma-separated values or range of values "
"(e.g., B<0-16,35,40->). Useful with option B<-I >from B<sar>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-j>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:156
msgid ""
"Print the contents of the data file in JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)  "
"format. Timestamps can be controlled by options B<-T >and B<-t>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:161
msgid ""
"Export the contents of the data file to a PCP (Performance Co-Pilot) "
"archive.  The name of the archive can be specified using the keyword "
"B<pcparchive= >with option B<-O>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-O >I<opts>B<[,...]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:167
msgid ""
"Use the specified options to control the output of B<sadf>.  The following "
"options are used to control SVG output displayed by B<sadf -g>:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:168
#, no-wrap
msgid "autoscale"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:175
msgid ""
"Draw all the graphs of a given view as large as possible based on current "
"view's scale. To do this, a factor (10, 100, 1000...) is used to enlarge the "
"graph drawing.  This option may be interesting when several graphs are drawn "
"on the same view, some with only very small values, and others with high "
"ones, the latter making the former hardly visible."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:175
#, no-wrap
msgid "bwcol"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:177
msgid "Use a black and white palette to draw the graphs."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:177
#, no-wrap
msgid "customcol"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:182
msgid ""
"Use a customizable color palette instead of the default one to draw the "
"graphs. See environment variable B<S_COLORS_PALETTE> below to know how to "
"customize that palette."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:182 original/man1/sadf.1:224
#, no-wrap
msgid "debug"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:184
msgid "Add helpful comments in SVG output file."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:184
#, no-wrap
msgid "height=I<value>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:188
msgid "Set SVG canvas height to I<value>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:188
#, no-wrap
msgid "oneday"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:193
msgid ""
"Display graphs data over a period of 24 hours. Note that hours are still "
"printed in UTC by default: You should use option B<-T >to print them in "
"local time and get a time window starting from midnight."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:193
#, no-wrap
msgid "packed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:195
msgid ""
"Group all views from the same activity (and for the same device) on the same "
"row."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "showidle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:197
msgid "Also display %idle state in graphs for CPU statistics."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "showinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:199
msgid ""
"Display additional information (such as the date and the host name) on each "
"view."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "showtoc"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:202
msgid ""
"Add a table of contents at the beginning of the SVG output, consisting of "
"links pointing at the first graph of each activity."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:202
#, no-wrap
msgid "skipempty"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:204
msgid "Do not display views where all graphs have only zero values."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:208
msgid ""
"The following option may be used when converting an old system activity "
"binary datafile to current up-to-date format:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:209
#, no-wrap
msgid "hz=I<value>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:212
msgid ""
"Specify the number of ticks per second for the machine where the old "
"datafile has been created."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:215
msgid "The following option may be used when data are exported to a PCP archive:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:216
#, no-wrap
msgid "pcparchive=I<name>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:219
msgid "Specify the name of the PCP archive to create."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:223
msgid "The following option is used to control raw output displayed by B<sadf -r>:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:226
msgid "Display additional information, mainly useful for debugging purpose."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:239
msgid ""
"Tell B<sadf> that processor dependent statistics are to be reported only for "
"the specified processor or processors.  I<cpu_list> is a list of "
"comma-separated values or range of values (e.g., B<0,2,4-7,12->).  Note that "
"processor 0 is the first processor, and processor B<all >is the global "
"average among all processors. Specifying the B<ALL> keyword reports "
"statistics for each individual processor, and globally for all processors."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:239
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-p>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:250
msgid ""
"Print the contents of the data file in a format that can easily be handled "
"by pattern processing commands like B<awk>.  The output consists of fields "
"separated by a tab. Each record contains the hostname of the host where the "
"file was created, the interval value (or -1 if not applicable), the "
"timestamp, the device name (or - if not applicable), the field name and its "
"value.  Note that timestamp output can be controlled by options B<-T>, B<-t "
">and B<-U>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:257
msgid ""
"Print the raw contents of the data file. With this format, the values for "
"all the counters are displayed as read from the kernel, which means e.g., "
"that no average values are calculated over the elapsed time interval.  "
"Output can be controlled using option B<-O >(see above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:259 original/man1/sar.1:1481
msgid "B<-s [ >I<hh>B<:>I<mm>B<[:>I<ss>B<] ]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:261 original/man1/sar.1:1483
msgid "B<-s [ >I<seconds_since_the_epoch >B<]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:265
msgid ""
"Set the starting time of the report. The default starting time is "
"08:00:00. Hours must be given in 24-hour format, or as the number of seconds "
"since the epoch (given as a 10 digit number)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:269
msgid "Display timestamp in local time instead of UTC (Coordinated Universal Time)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:273
msgid ""
"Display timestamp in the original local time of the data file creator "
"instead of UTC (Coordinated Universal Time)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:287
msgid ""
"Print the contents of the data file in XML format.  Timestamps can be "
"controlled by options B<-T >and B<-t>.  The corresponding DTD (Document Type "
"Definition) and XML Schema are included in the sysstat source package. They "
"are also available at I<https://sysstat.github.io/>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:291
msgid "The B<sadf> command takes into account the following environment variables:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:291
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<S_COLORS_PALETTE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:301
msgid ""
"Specify the colors used by B<sadf -g> to render the SVG output. This "
"environment variable is taken into account only when the custom color "
"palette has been selected with the option B<customcol >(see option B<-O>).  "
"Its value is a colon-separated list of capabilities associated with "
"six-digit, three-byte hexadecimal numbers (hex triplets) representing colors "
"that defaults to"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:303
msgid "B<0=000000:1=1a1aff:2=1affb2:3=b21aff:>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:305
msgid "B<4=1ab2ff:5=ff1a1a:6=ffb31a:7=b2ff1a:>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:307
msgid "B<8=efefef:9=000000:A=1a1aff:B=1affb2:>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:309
msgid "B<C=b21aff:D=1ab2ff:E=ff1a1a:F=ffb31a:>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:311
msgid "B<G=bebebe:H=000000:I=000000:K=ffffff:>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:313
msgid "B<L=000000:T=000000:W=000000:X=000000>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:318
msgid ""
"Capabilities consisting of a hexadecimal digit (B<0 >through B<F>) are used "
"to specify the first sixteen colors in the palette (these colors are used to "
"draw the graphs), e.g., 3=ffffff would indicate that the third color in the "
"palette is white (0xffffff)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:320
msgid "Other capabilities are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:321
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<G=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:324
msgid "Specify the color used to draw the grid lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:324
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<H=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:327
msgid "Specify the color used to display the report header."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:330
msgid ""
"Specify the color used to display additional information (e.g., date, "
"hostname...)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:330
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<K=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:333
msgid "Specify the color used for the graphs background."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:333
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<L=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:336
msgid ""
"Specify the default color (which is for example used to display the table of "
"contents)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:336
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<T=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:339
msgid "Specify the color used to display the graphs title."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:339
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<W=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:342
msgid "Specify the color used to display warning and error messages."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:342
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<X=>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:345
msgid "Specify the color used to draw the axes and display the graduations."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:346 original/man1/sar.1:1677
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<S_TIME_DEF_TIME>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:354
msgid ""
"If this variable exists and its value is B<UTC >then B<sadf> will use UTC "
"time instead of local time to determine the current daily data file located "
"in the I</var/log/sa> directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:356
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<sadf -d /var/log/sa/sa21 -- -r -n DEV>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:361
msgid ""
"Extract memory and network statistics from system activity file I<sa21>, and "
"display them in a format that can be ingested by a database."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sadf.1:361
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<sadf -p -P 1>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:366
msgid ""
"Extract CPU statistics for processor 1 (the second processor) from current "
"daily data file, and display them in a format that can easily be handled by "
"a pattern processing command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:374
msgid ""
"SVG output (as created by option B<-g>)  is fully compliant with SVG 1.1 "
"standard.  Graphics have been successfully displayed in various web "
"browsers, including Firefox, Chrome and Opera. Yet SVG rendering is broken "
"on Microsoft browsers (tested on Internet Explorer 11 and Edge 13.1): So "
"please don't use them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:377 original/man1/sar.1:1727
msgid "I</var/log/sa/saDD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:379 original/man1/sar.1:1729
msgid "I</var/log/sa/saYYYYMMDD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:383 original/man1/sar.1:1733
msgid ""
"The standard system activity daily data files and their default location.  "
"I<YYYY >stands for the current year, I<MM >for the current month and I<DD> "
"for the current day."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sadf.1:390
msgid "B<sar>(1), B<sadc>(8), B<sa1>(8), B<sa2>(8), B<sysstat>(5)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: original/man1/sar.1:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "SAR"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:5
msgid "sar - Collect, report, or save system activity information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:18
msgid ""
"B<sar [ -A ] [ -B ] [ -b ] [ -C ] [ -D ] [ -d ] [ -F [ MOUNT ] ] [ -H ] [ -h "
"] [ -p ]> B<[ -r [ ALL ] ] [ -S ] [ -t ] [ -u [ ALL ] ] [ -V ] [ -v ] [ -W ] "
"[ -w ] [ -x ] [ -y ] [ -z ]> B<[ --dec={ 0 | 1 | 2 } ]> B<[ "
"--dev=>I<dev_list >B<] [ --fs=>I<fs_list >B<] [ --help ] [ --human ] [ "
"--iface=>I<iface_list> B<] [ --int=>I<int_list >B<] [ --pretty ] [ --sadc ] "
"[ -I [ SUM | ALL ] ] [ -P { >I<cpu_list> B<| ALL } ] [ -m {> "
"I<keyword>B<[,...] | ALL } ] [ -n { >I<keyword>B<[,...] | ALL } ] [ -q [ "
">I<keyword>B<[,...] | ALL ] ]> B<[ -j { SID | ID | LABEL | PATH | UUID | "
"... } ]> B<[ -f [ >I<filename >B<] | -o [ >I<filename >B<] | -[0-9]+ ]> B<[ "
"-i >I<interval >B<] [ -s [ >I<start_time > B<] ] [ -e [ >I<end_time >B<] ] ] "
"[ >I<interval >B<[ >I<count >B<] ]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:43
msgid ""
"The B<sar> command writes to standard output the contents of selected "
"cumulative activity counters in the operating system. The accounting system, "
"based on the values in the I<count >and I<interval> parameters, writes "
"information the specified number of times spaced at the specified intervals "
"in seconds.  If the I<interval >parameter is set to zero, the B<sar> command "
"displays the average statistics for the time since the system was "
"started. If the I<interval >parameter is specified without the I<count> "
"parameter, then reports are generated continuously.  The collected data can "
"also be saved in the file specified by the B<-o >I<filename> flag, in "
"addition to being displayed onto the screen. If I<filename >is omitted, "
"B<sar> uses the standard system activity daily data file (see below).  By "
"default all the data available from the kernel are saved in the data file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:53
msgid ""
"The B<sar> command extracts and writes to standard output records previously "
"saved in a file. This file can be either the one specified by the B<-f> flag "
"or, by default, the standard system activity daily data file.  It is also "
"possible to enter B<-1>, B<-2 >etc. as an argument to B<sar> to display data "
"of that days ago. For example, B<-1 >will point at the standard system "
"activity file of yesterday."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:71
msgid ""
"Standard system activity daily data files are named I<saDD >or "
"I<saYYYYMMDD>, where I<YYYY >stands for the current year, I<MM >for the "
"current month and I<DD> for the current day. They are the default files used "
"by B<sar> only when no filename has been explicitly specified.  When used to "
"write data to files (with its option B<-o>), B<sar> will use I<saYYYYMMDD> "
"if option B<-D> has also been specified, else it will use I<saDD>.  When "
"used to display the records previously saved in a file, B<sar> will look for "
"the most recent of I<saDD >and I<saYYYYMMDD>, and use it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:79
msgid ""
"Standard system activity daily data files are located in the I</var/log/sa> "
"directory by default. Yet it is possible to specify an alternate location "
"for them: If a directory (instead of a plain file) is used with options B<-f "
">or B<-o> then it will be considered as the directory containing the data "
"files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:90
msgid ""
"Without the B<-P >flag, the B<sar> command reports system-wide (global among "
"all processors) statistics, which are calculated as averages for values "
"expressed as percentages, and as sums otherwise. If the B<-P >flag is given, "
"the B<sar> command reports activity which relates to the specified processor "
"or processors. If B<-P ALL >is given, the B<sar> command reports statistics "
"for each individual processor and global statistics among all "
"processors. Offline processors are not displayed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:96
msgid ""
"You can select information about specific system activities using flags. Not "
"specifying any flags selects only CPU activity.  Specifying the B<-A> flag "
"selects all possible activities."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:103
msgid ""
"The default version of the B<sar> command (CPU utilization report) might be "
"one of the first facilities the user runs to begin system activity "
"investigation, because it monitors major system resources. If CPU "
"utilization is near 100 percent (user + nice + system), the workload sampled "
"is CPU-bound."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:108
msgid ""
"If multiple samples and multiple reports are desired, it is convenient to "
"specify an output file for the B<sar >command. Run the B<sar> command as a "
"background process. The syntax for this is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:110
msgid "B<sar -o >I<datafile interval count >B<E<gt>/dev/null 2E<gt>&1 &>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:120
msgid ""
"All data are captured in binary form and saved to a file (I<datafile>).  The "
"data can then be selectively displayed with the B<sar >command using the "
"B<-f >option. Set the I<interval >and I<count >parameters to select I<count "
">records at I<interval> second intervals. If the I<count> parameter is not "
"set, all the records saved in the file will be selected.  Collection of data "
"in this manner is useful to characterize system usage over a period of time "
"and determine peak usage hours."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:123
msgid "Note: The B<sar> command only reports on local activities."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:132
msgid ""
"This is equivalent to specifying B<-bBdFHISvwWy -m ALL -n ALL -q ALL -r ALL "
"-u ALL>.  This option also implies specifying B<-I ALL -P ALL> unless these "
"options are explicitly set on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-B>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:136
msgid "Report paging statistics.  The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:137
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgpgin/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:139
msgid "Total number of kibibytes the system paged in from disk per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:139
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgpgout/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:141
msgid "Total number of kibibytes the system paged out to disk per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:141
#, no-wrap
msgid "fault/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:145
msgid ""
"Number of page faults (major + minor) made by the system per second.  This "
"is not a count of page faults that generate I/O, because some page faults "
"can be resolved without I/O."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:148
msgid ""
"Number of major faults the system has made per second, those which have "
"required loading a memory page from disk."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgfree/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:150
msgid "Number of pages placed on the free list by the system per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:150
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgscank/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:153
msgid ""
"Number of pages scanned by the kswapd daemon per second (done in background, "
"healthy system)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:153
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgscand/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:155
msgid "Number of pages scanned directly per second (process is stalled)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:155
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgsteal/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:158
msgid ""
"Number of pages the system has reclaimed from cache (pagecache and "
"swapcache) per second to satisfy its memory demands."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgprom/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:161
msgid ""
"Number of pages promoted (i.e. migrated from slow to fast memory types)  by "
"the system per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgdem/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:164
msgid ""
"Number of pages demoted (i.e. migrated from fast to slow memory types)  by "
"the system per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:165
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-b>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:168
msgid "Report I/O and transfer rate statistics. The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:174 original/man1/sar.1:223
msgid ""
"Total number of transfers per second that were issued to physical devices.  "
"A transfer is an I/O request to a physical device. Multiple logical requests "
"can be combined into a single I/O request to the device.  A transfer is of "
"indeterminate size."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:174
#, no-wrap
msgid "rtps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:176
msgid "Total number of read requests per second issued to physical devices."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:176
#, no-wrap
msgid "wtps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:178
msgid "Total number of write requests per second issued to physical devices."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:178
#, no-wrap
msgid "dtps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:180
msgid "Total number of discard requests per second issued to physical devices."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:180
#, no-wrap
msgid "bread/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:183
msgid ""
"Total amount of data read from the devices in blocks per second.  Blocks are "
"equivalent to sectors and therefore have a size of 512 bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:183
#, no-wrap
msgid "bwrtn/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:185
msgid "Total amount of data written to devices in blocks per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "bdscd/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:187
msgid "Total amount of data discarded for devices in blocks per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:192
msgid ""
"When reading data from a file, tell B<sar >to display comments that have "
"been inserted by B<sadc>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:192
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-D>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:198
msgid ""
"Use I<saYYYYMMDD >instead of I<saDD> as the standard system activity daily "
"data file name. This option works only when used in conjunction with option "
"B<-o >to save data to file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:217
msgid ""
"Report activity for each block device.  When data are displayed, the device "
"name is displayed as it (should) appear in I</dev>.  B<sar >uses data in "
"I</sys >to determine the device name based on its major and minor numbers.  "
"If this name resolution fails, B<sar >will use name mapping controlled by "
"I</etc/sysconfig/sysstat.ioconf >file.  Persistent device names can also be "
"printed if option B<-j> is used (see below). Statistics for all devices are "
"displayed unless a restricted list is specified using option B<--dev=> (see "
"corresponding option entry).  Note that disk activity depends on B<sadc>'s "
"options B<-S DISK >and B<-S XDISK> to be collected. The following values are "
"displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:223
#, no-wrap
msgid "rkB/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:225
msgid "Number of kibibytes read from the device per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:225
#, no-wrap
msgid "wkB/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:227
msgid "Number of kibibytes written to the device per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:227
#, no-wrap
msgid "dkB/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:229
msgid "Number of kibibytes discarded for the device per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:247
msgid ""
"Percentage of elapsed time during which I/O requests were issued to the "
"device (bandwidth utilization for the device). Device saturation occurs when "
"this value is close to 100% for devices serving requests serially. But for "
"devices serving requests in parallel, such as RAID arrays and modern SSDs, "
"this number does not reflect their performance limits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:256
msgid ""
"Specify the block devices for which statistics are to be displayed by "
"B<sar>.  I<dev_list >is a list of comma-separated device names."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:268
msgid ""
"Set the ending time of the report. The default ending time is "
"18:00:00. Hours must be given in 24-hour format, or as the number of seconds "
"since the epoch (given as a 10 digit number).  This option can be used when "
"data are read from or written to a file (options B<-f >or B<-o>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:269
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-F [ MOUNT ]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:284
msgid ""
"Display statistics for currently mounted filesystems. Pseudo-filesystems are "
"ignored. At the end of the report, B<sar> will display a summary of all "
"those filesystems. Use of the B<MOUNT> parameter keyword indicates that "
"mountpoint will be reported instead of filesystem device. Statistics for all "
"filesystems are displayed unless a restricted list is specified using option "
"B<--fs=> (see corresponding option entry).  Note that filesystems statistics "
"depend on B<sadc>'s option B<-S XDISK > to be collected."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:286
msgid "The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:287
#, no-wrap
msgid "MBfsfree"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:289
msgid ""
"Total amount of free space in mebibytes (including space available only to "
"privileged user)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:289
#, no-wrap
msgid "MBfsused"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:291
msgid "Total amount of space used in mebibytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:291
#, no-wrap
msgid "%fsused"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:293
msgid "Percentage of filesystem space used, as seen by a privileged user."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:293
#, no-wrap
msgid "%ufsused"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:295
msgid "Percentage of filesystem space used, as seen by an unprivileged user."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:295
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ifree"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:297
msgid "Total number of free file nodes in filesystem."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:297
#, no-wrap
msgid "Iused"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:299
msgid "Total number of file nodes used in filesystem."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:299
#, no-wrap
msgid "%Iused"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:301
msgid "Percentage of file nodes used in filesystem."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:302
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-f [ >I<filename >B<]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:314
msgid ""
"Extract records from I<filename >(created by the B<-o >I<filename> "
"flag). The default value of the I<filename> parameter is the current "
"standard system activity daily data file. If I<filename> is a directory "
"instead of a plain file then it is considered as the directory where the "
"standard system activity daily data files are located. Option B<-f >is "
"exclusive of option B<-o>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:326
msgid ""
"Specify the filesystems for which statistics are to be displayed by B<sar>.  "
"I<fs_list> is a list of comma-separated filesystem device names (for example "
"I</dev/sdb1>)  and/or mountpoints (for example I</srv>).  Either form is "
"accepted for filtering, independent of whether option B<-F> is showing "
"device names or mountpoints."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:330
msgid ""
"Report hugepages utilization statistics.  The following values are "
"displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:331
#, no-wrap
msgid "kbhugfree"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:333
msgid "Amount of hugepages memory in kibibytes that is not yet allocated."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:333
#, no-wrap
msgid "kbhugused"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:335
msgid "Amount of hugepages memory in kibibytes that has been allocated."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:335
#, no-wrap
msgid "%hugused"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:337
msgid "Percentage of total hugepages memory that has been allocated."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:337
#, no-wrap
msgid "kbhugrsvd"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:339
msgid "Amount of reserved hugepages memory in kibibytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:339
#, no-wrap
msgid "kbhugsurp"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:341
msgid "Amount of surplus hugepages memory in kibibytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:346
msgid "This option is equivalent to specifying B<--pretty --human>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:346
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--help>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:349
msgid "Display a short help message then exit."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:354
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-I [ SUM | ALL ]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:370
msgid ""
"Report statistics for interrupts. The values displayed are the number of "
"interrupts per second for the given processor or among all processors.  A "
"list of interrupts can be specified using B<--int=> (see this option). The "
"B<SUM> keyword indicates that the total number of interrupts received per "
"second is to be displayed. The B<ALL> keyword indicates that statistics from "
"all interrupts are to be reported (this is the default).  Note that "
"interrupts statistics depend on B<sadc>'s option B<-S INT> to be collected."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:370
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-i >I<interval>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:374
msgid ""
"Select data records at seconds as close as possible to the number specified "
"by the I<interval >parameter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:380
msgid ""
"Specify the network interfaces for which statistics are to be displayed by "
"B<sar>.  I<iface_list> is a list of comma-separated interface names."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:387
msgid ""
"Specify the interrupts names for which statistics are to be displayed by "
"B<sar>.  I<int_list> is a list of comma-separated values or range of values "
"(e.g., B<0-16,35,40->)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:387
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-j { SID | ID | LABEL | PATH | UUID | ... }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:399
msgid ""
"Display persistent device names. Use this option in conjunction with option "
"B<-d>. Keywords B<ID>, B<LABEL>, etc. specify the type of the persistent "
"name. These keywords are not limited, only prerequisite is that directory "
"with required persistent names is present in I</dev/disk>.  Keyword B<SID> "
"tries to get a stable identifier to use as the device name. A stable "
"identifier won't change across reboots for the same physical device. If it "
"exists, this identifier is normally the WWN (World Wide Name) of the device, "
"as read from the I</dev/disk/by-id >directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:399
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-m { >I<keyword>B<[,...] | ALL }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:404
msgid ""
"Report power management statistics.  Note that these statistics depend on "
"B<sadc>'s option B<-S POWER >to be collected."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:407
msgid ""
"Possible keywords are B<BAT>, B<CPU>, B<FAN>, B<FREQ>, B<IN>, B<TEMP >and "
"B<USB>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:411
msgid ""
"With the B<BAT> keyword, statistics about batteries capacity are reported.  "
"The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:412
#, no-wrap
msgid "%cap"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:414
msgid "Battery capacity."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:414
#, no-wrap
msgid "cap/min"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:416
msgid "Capacity lost or gained per minute by the battery."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:416
#, no-wrap
msgid "status"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:418
msgid ""
"Charging status of the battery: ↑ (full), ↗ (charging), → (not charging), ↘ "
"(discharging), ? (unknown)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:424
msgid ""
"With the B<CPU> keyword, statistics about CPU are reported.  The following "
"value is displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:425
#, no-wrap
msgid "MHz"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:427
msgid "Instantaneous CPU clock frequency in MHz."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:433
msgid ""
"With the B<FAN> keyword, statistics about fans speed are reported.  The "
"following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:434
#, no-wrap
msgid "rpm"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:436
msgid "Fan speed expressed in revolutions per minute."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:436
#, no-wrap
msgid "drpm"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:439
msgid ""
"This field is calculated as the difference between current fan speed (rpm)  "
"and its low limit (fan_min)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:439 original/man1/sar.1:465 original/man1/sar.1:479
#, no-wrap
msgid "DEVICE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:441 original/man1/sar.1:467 original/man1/sar.1:481
msgid "Sensor device name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:447
msgid ""
"With the B<FREQ> keyword, statistics about CPU clock frequency are "
"reported.  The following value is displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:448
#, no-wrap
msgid "wghMHz"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:452
msgid ""
"Weighted average CPU clock frequency in MHz.  Note that the cpufreq-stats "
"driver must be compiled in the kernel for this option to work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:458
msgid ""
"With the B<IN> keyword, statistics about voltage inputs are reported.  The "
"following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:459
#, no-wrap
msgid "inV"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:461
msgid "Voltage input expressed in Volts."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:461
#, no-wrap
msgid "%in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:465
msgid ""
"Relative input value. A value of 100% means that voltage input has reached "
"its high limit (in_max) whereas a value of 0% means that it has reached its "
"low limit (in_min)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:473
msgid ""
"With the B<TEMP> keyword, statistics about devices temperature are "
"reported.  The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:474
#, no-wrap
msgid "degC"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:476
msgid "Device temperature expressed in degrees Celsius."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:476
#, no-wrap
msgid "%temp"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:479
msgid ""
"Relative device temperature. A value of 100% means that temperature has "
"reached its high limit (temp_max)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:490
msgid ""
"With the B<USB >keyword, the B<sar> command takes a snapshot of all the USB "
"devices currently plugged into the system. At the end of the report, B<sar> "
"will display a summary of all those USB devices.  The following values are "
"displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:491
#, no-wrap
msgid "BUS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:493
msgid "Root hub number of the USB device."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:493
#, no-wrap
msgid "idvendor"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:495
msgid "Vendor ID number (assigned by USB organization)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:495
#, no-wrap
msgid "idprod"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:497
msgid "Product ID number (assigned by Manufacturer)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:497
#, no-wrap
msgid "maxpower"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:499
msgid "Maximum power consumption of the device (expressed in mA)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:499
#, no-wrap
msgid "manufact"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:501
msgid "Manufacturer name."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:501
#, no-wrap
msgid "product"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:503
msgid "Product name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:509
msgid ""
"The B<ALL> keyword is equivalent to specifying all the keywords above and "
"therefore all the power management statistics are reported."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man1/sar.1:509
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-n { >I<keyword>B<[,...] | ALL }>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:512
msgid "Report network statistics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:517
msgid ""
"Possible keywords are B<DEV>, B<EDEV>, B<FC>, B<ICMP>, B<EICMP>, B<ICMP6>, "
"B<EICMP6>, B<IP>, B<EIP>, B<IP6>, B<EIP6>, B<NFS>, B<NFSD>, B<SOCK>, "
"B<SOCK6>, B<SOFT>, B<TCP>, B<ETCP>, B<UDP >and B<UDP6>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:525
msgid ""
"With the B<DEV> keyword, statistics from the network devices are reported.  "
"Statistics for all network interfaces are displayed unless a restricted list "
"is specified using option B<--iface=> (see corresponding option entry).  The "
"following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:526 original/man1/sar.1:557
#, no-wrap
msgid "IFACE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:528 original/man1/sar.1:559
msgid "Name of the network interface for which statistics are reported."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:528
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxpck/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:530
msgid "Total number of packets received per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:530
#, no-wrap
msgid "txpck/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:532
msgid "Total number of packets transmitted per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:532
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxkB/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:534
msgid "Total number of kibibytes received per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:534
#, no-wrap
msgid "txkB/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:536
msgid "Total number of kibibytes transmitted per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:536
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxcmp/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:538
msgid "Number of compressed packets received per second (for cslip etc.)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:538
#, no-wrap
msgid "txcmp/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:540
msgid "Number of compressed packets transmitted per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:540
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxmcst/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:542
msgid "Number of multicast packets received per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:542
#, no-wrap
msgid "%ifutil"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:546
msgid ""
"Utilization percentage of the network interface. For half-duplex interfaces, "
"utilization is calculated using the sum of rxkB/s and txkB/s as a percentage "
"of the interface speed. For full-duplex, this is the greater of rxkB/S or "
"txkB/s."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:556
msgid ""
"With the B<EDEV> keyword, statistics on failures (errors) from the network "
"devices are reported.  Statistics for all network interfaces are displayed "
"unless a restricted list is specified using option B<--iface=> (see "
"corresponding option entry).  The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:559
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxerr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:561
msgid "Total number of bad packets received per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:561
#, no-wrap
msgid "txerr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:563
msgid "Total number of errors that happened per second while transmitting packets."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:563
#, no-wrap
msgid "coll/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:565
msgid "Number of collisions that happened per second while transmitting packets."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:565
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxdrop/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:567
msgid ""
"Number of received packets dropped per second because of a lack of space in "
"linux buffers."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:567
#, no-wrap
msgid "txdrop/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:569
msgid ""
"Number of transmitted packets dropped per second because of a lack of space "
"in linux buffers."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:569
#, no-wrap
msgid "txcarr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:571
msgid ""
"Number of carrier-errors that happened per second while transmitting "
"packets."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:571
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxfram/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:573
msgid ""
"Number of frame alignment errors that happened per second on received "
"packets."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:573
#, no-wrap
msgid "rxfifo/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:575
msgid "Number of FIFO overrun errors that happened per second on received packets."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:575
#, no-wrap
msgid "txfifo/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:577
msgid ""
"Number of FIFO overrun errors that happened per second on transmitted "
"packets."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:586
msgid ""
"With the B<FC> keyword, statistics about fibre channel traffic are "
"reported.  Note that fibre channel statistics depend on B<sadc>'s option "
"B<-S DISK> to be collected.  The following values are displayed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:587
#, no-wrap
msgid "FCHOST"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:589
msgid ""
"Name of the fibre channel host bus adapter (HBA) interface for which "
"statistics are reported."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:589
#, no-wrap
msgid "fch_rxf/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:591
msgid "The total number of frames received per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:591
#, no-wrap
msgid "fch_txf/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:593
msgid "The total number of frames transmitted per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:593
#, no-wrap
msgid "fch_rxw/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:595
msgid "The total number of transmission words received per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:595
#, no-wrap
msgid "fch_txw/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:597
msgid "The total number of transmission words transmitted per second."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:607
msgid ""
"With the B<ICMP> keyword, statistics about ICMPv4 network traffic are "
"reported.  Note that ICMPv4 statistics depend on B<sadc>'s option B<-S SNMP> "
"to be collected.  The following values are displayed (formal SNMP names "
"between square brackets):"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:608
#, no-wrap
msgid "imsg/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:612
msgid ""
"The total number of ICMP messages which the entity received per second "
"[icmpInMsgs].  Note that this counter includes all those counted by ierr/s."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:612
#, no-wrap
msgid "omsg/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:616
msgid ""
"The total number of ICMP messages which this entity attempted to send per "
"second [icmpOutMsgs].  Note that this counter includes all those counted by "
"oerr/s."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:616
#, no-wrap
msgid "iech/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:618
msgid ""
"The number of ICMP Echo (request) messages received per second "
"[icmpInEchos]."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:618
#, no-wrap
msgid "iechr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:620
msgid "The number of ICMP Echo Reply messages received per second [icmpInEchoReps]."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:620
#, no-wrap
msgid "oech/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:622
msgid "The number of ICMP Echo (request) messages sent per second [icmpOutEchos]."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:622
#, no-wrap
msgid "oechr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:624
msgid "The number of ICMP Echo Reply messages sent per second [icmpOutEchoReps]."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:624
#, no-wrap
msgid "itm/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:626
msgid ""
"The number of ICMP Timestamp (request) messages received per second "
"[icmpInTimestamps]."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/sar.1:626
#, no-wrap
msgid "itmr/s"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/sar.1:628
msgid ""
"The number of ICMP Timestamp Reply messages received per second "
"[icmpInTimestampReps]."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
